WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.508 --> 00:00:07.450
"Grandpa, we're home!"

2
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:18.334
Laurie shouted as she ran up the stairs.

3
00:00:19.055 --> 00:00:23.376
She had just come back from the beach with her dad and was carrying a jar full of

4
00:00:23.456 --> 00:00:30.459
seashells. "Choose any one you like, but only one," she said, standing at the study door.

5
00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:31.920
"Thank you!"

6
00:00:32.436 --> 00:00:35.277
Grandpa said, examining the shells closely.

7
00:00:35.897 --> 00:00:38.378
He took a small peach-colored shell from the jar.

8
00:00:39.158 --> 00:00:40.878
"I'll add this to my collection."

9
00:00:41.699 --> 00:00:43.419
"You have a shell collection too?"

10
00:00:43.879 --> 00:00:44.599
Laurie asked.

11
00:00:45.279 --> 00:00:50.801
"Well, I have just two shells, but they are very special ones from Lebanon!"

12
00:00:51.261 --> 00:00:56.743
Grandpa reached high on a shelf and took down a bowl with two spiny seashells in it.

13
00:00:57.563 --> 00:01:01.864
"These are sea-snail shells from the Mediterranean coastline.

14
00:01:02.672 --> 00:01:07.734
I found them when I visited Lebanon last year to research the ancient Phoenicians.

15
00:01:08.854 --> 00:01:17.256
They were seafaring merchants who lived there from around 2000 BC to 500 BC, when they were taken

16
00:01:17.376 --> 00:01:19.077
over by the Persian Empire."

17
00:01:19.097 --> 00:01:21.277
"What are seafaring merchants?"

18
00:01:21.838 --> 00:01:22.438
said Laurie.

19
00:01:22.458 --> 00:01:29.660
"People who spend a lot of time sailing at sea and make their living by trading things," Grandpa

20
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:38.421
explained. "The coasts of Lebanon are surrounded by high, rugged mountains, which protected Phoenicia, but it also made

21
00:01:38.461 --> 00:01:40.243
it difficult to travel by land.

22
00:01:41.043 --> 00:01:46.827
So, out of necessity, the Phoenicians became great shipbuilders and seafarers.

23
00:01:47.528 --> 00:01:53.672
From their coastal cities, they sailed to other Middle Eastern cities, and to Africa and Europe.

24
00:01:54.493 --> 00:01:58.896
There, they traded their goods for things they couldn't find in their homeland.

25
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:03.741
They are considered the first organized traders and merchants of the world.

26
00:02:04.662 --> 00:02:11.044
Even though we call them Phoenicians and the area they lived in Phoenicia, they weren't really united into

27
00:02:11.084 --> 00:02:12.424
a country or empire.

28
00:02:13.024 --> 00:02:17.626
Phoenicia was more like individual cities that developed close to one another.

29
00:02:18.386 --> 00:02:24.868
But when the Phoenicians traveled around selling their goods, they became known as one people, the Phoenicians."

30
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.742
"Did they simply sell these seashells by the seashore?"

31
00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.004
Laurie asked, giggling.

32
00:02:33.124 --> 00:02:41.309
"No, silly, but the sea snails that came from these seashells—whew!— were the source of the Phoenicians' most

33
00:02:41.409 --> 00:02:43.610
valuable item: purple dye.

34
00:02:44.151 --> 00:02:50.894
Purple dye hadn't yet been discovered anywhere else, and when the Phoenicians began to make cloth dyed this

35
00:02:51.115 --> 00:02:53.456
wonderful purple, people loved it.

36
00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:58.708
But hundreds of snails were needed to dye even a small piece of fabric.

37
00:02:59.488 --> 00:03:06.074
So the dye-making process was very time-consuming, and therefore, the dye was worth a lot of money.

38
00:03:07.295 --> 00:03:13.340
It was so expensive that only royalty or the very wealthy could afford anything purple.

39
00:03:13.961 --> 00:03:17.363
That's how purple became known as the 'royal' color.

40
00:03:18.304 --> 00:03:22.348
Glass and cedarwood were also popular Phoenician items.

41
00:03:23.128 --> 00:03:30.610
Cedar, which grew in the mountains of Lebanon, was rare in neighboring lands; that made it highly desirable.

42
00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:35.752
It was also perfect for building and produced great-smelling oils.

43
00:03:36.492 --> 00:03:43.374
The Egyptians in particular bought lots of cedar, which they used to build temples, palaces, and coffins.

44
00:03:44.414 --> 00:03:51.356
The Phoenicians' seaside location helped them with something else—glassmaking, because glass is made with sand.

45
00:03:52.236 --> 00:03:57.958
People think Egyptians had been making glass for a while, but the Phoenicians developed it into an art.

46
00:03:58.759 --> 00:04:03.661
They made colored beads, clear vases, and other beautiful glasswork.

47
00:04:04.941 --> 00:04:07.742
We owe a lot to the Phoenicians for their creations.

48
00:04:08.363 --> 00:04:11.324
But they are most famous for one thing.

49
00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:17.146
You know, as merchants, it was important for them to keep records of all their sales.

50
00:04:18.107 --> 00:04:20.868
What do you think they invented to help them do this?"

51
00:04:21.364 --> 00:04:22.385
Grandpa paused.

52
00:04:22.405 --> 00:04:24.806
"Hmm receipts?"

53
00:04:25.406 --> 00:04:26.126
Laurie guessed.

54
00:04:27.227 --> 00:04:33.790
"Well, yes, but how would they write receipts if they didn't have an alphabet?

55
00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:39.252
The Phoenicians invented the first 22-letter alphabet!

56
00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:47.996
Of course, written language had gradually been developing since the time of picture symbols, like hieroglyphics, but the

57
00:04:48.056 --> 00:04:52.397
Phoenicians created the first letters representing sound alone.

58
00:04:53.178 --> 00:04:55.998
We can even trace our alphabet back to theirs!

59
00:04:56.518 --> 00:04:58.419
Here, what do you think this says?"

60
00:04:59.619 --> 00:05:02.920
Grandpa wrote three Phoenician letters on a piece of paper.

61
00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:11.122
"Um, triangle, circle, cross D-O-T!

62
00:05:11.763 --> 00:05:14.383
Dot!" Laurie cried excitedly.

63
00:05:14.403 --> 00:05:18.249
"Amazing! You can read Phoenician!"

64
00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:21.270
Grandpa laughed.

65
00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.171
He handed her an open book from his desk.

66
00:05:24.891 --> 00:05:27.512
The page showed a chart of the Phoenician alphabet.

67
00:05:27.532 --> 00:05:32.675
"Wow. Letters have come a long way in three thousand years.

68
00:05:33.375 --> 00:05:36.316
I wonder what they'll look like three thousand years from now!"

69
00:05:36.816 --> 00:05:37.497
Laurie said.

70
00:05:38.217 --> 00:05:39.937
She flipped to another page in the book.

71
00:05:39.958 --> 00:05:42.579
"Ooh, who is this?

72
00:05:43.219 --> 00:05:44.699
She's beautiful!"

73
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:45.820
she whispered.

74
00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.381
"That's Jezebel, princess of Tyre.

75
00:05:49.801 --> 00:05:52.482
Tyre was a city on Phoenicia's coast.

76
00:05:53.202 --> 00:05:56.663
It was one of the richest, most powerful Phoenician cities.

77
00:05:57.723 --> 00:06:03.285
The king of Tyre married his beautiful daughter to the king of Israel's son, Ahab.

78
00:06:03.905 --> 00:06:09.307
He thought this would keep good relations between the two neighboring peoples, but he was wrong.

79
00:06:10.007 --> 00:06:18.311
In Israel, the Jewish people worshipped one god, while Phoenicians worshipped a number of gods and goddesses, who

80
00:06:18.331 --> 00:06:25.575
they believed controlled weather, harvests, the sea, the moon, and so on—in other words, the natural world.

81
00:06:26.415 --> 00:06:32.758
Now, Jezebel was a passionate woman who loved her husband but loved her native religion more.

82
00:06:33.699 --> 00:06:40.002
She asked Ahab to build temples to her gods and goddesses in his land, and she encouraged the

83
00:06:40.062 --> 00:06:42.544
people of Israel to join her in worship.

84
00:06:43.188 --> 00:06:44.829
The Jewish leaders got upset.

85
00:06:45.429 --> 00:06:49.110
They destroyed the Phoenician temples and killed the Phoenician priests.

86
00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:54.632
In all the political turmoil, Ahab and Jezebel were also killed."

87
00:06:56.672 --> 00:06:57.672
Grandpa sighed.

88
00:06:57.692 --> 00:07:02.514
"Wow! What a story for a beautiful Saturday afternoon!"

89
00:07:02.534 --> 00:07:07.776
"Oh! Why do strong women in history always die tragically?"

90
00:07:08.296 --> 00:07:09.116
Laurie cried.

91
00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.904
"One day soon, I'll have a story for you about a strong woman who lives to see a

92
00:07:15.024 --> 00:07:17.046
happy end," said Grandpa.

93
00:07:17.066 --> 00:07:21.470
"Well, I'm going to put this stuff in my room now.

94
00:07:22.631 --> 00:07:27.515
But I'll always think of crushed sea snails when I wear my purple tank top and of that

95
00:07:27.735 --> 00:07:30.297
ancient, funny-looking alphabet when I'm writing!

96
00:07:31.198 --> 00:07:33.019
And you can have another seashell.

97
00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:34.981
Catch!" Laurie said.

98
00:07:35.622 --> 00:07:39.674
She tossed another shell from her jar to Grandpa and walked out the door.
